            "Conical, slightly curved tooth with facets and sharp carinae, 2 to 8 cm, often still attached to part of the root or jaw; glossy brown, black or grey enamel. Apatite (calcium phosphate); enamel hardness about 5; specific gravity 2.9 to 3.1. Moroccan teeth are often sold in their phosphate matrix; many “complete jaws” are assemblies of added teeth and plaster or resin: check before buying. Set as pendants, they are popular with collectors.",
            "Morocco: the Oulad Abdoun phosphate basins near Khouribga, among the richest Late Cretaceous marine vertebrate deposits in the world, with several mosasaur species (Prognathodon, Mosasaurus, Halisaurus, Globidens with round crushing teeth). Also the Netherlands and Belgium (Maastricht chalk), the United States (Kansas, South Dakota), Angola and New Zealand.",
